<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · siege</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siege of Leningrad</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1941">1941</a>–<a href="/years/1944">1944</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The siege of Leningrad was a military <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> of the Soviet city of Leningrad, present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> on the Eastern Front of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>; it lasted 872 days, and the city was never captured. Land routes to the rest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> were cut on 8 September 1941, and the Germans decided to bomb the city and starve its inhabitants. Many residents starved during the winter of 1941–1942; the siege caused an estimated 1.5 million deaths, from a prewar population of 3.2 million, and some historians have classified it as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>. Supplies came across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-ladoga">Lake Ladoga</a>, in winter over the Road of Life on the ice, until the siege was fully broken on 27 January 1944.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/siege-of-leningrad.jpg" alt="Siege of Leningrad" width="330" height="242" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Boris Kudoyarov · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anti_aircraft_Leningrad_1941.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
